From 7980f604453799c62e5c54482c302870a1d95eb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nvr <nvr@localhost>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:50:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] NVR extension 1.8-rt: ray tracing core (ID3D9NvrExtRt):
 geometry staging for the 64-bit ray tracing helper, opt-in in-process
 acceleration structures

Windows' 32-bit Vulkan drivers (AMD 25.10, NVIDIA 582; measured 2026-10-28) offer no ray tracing,
so NVR traces in a 64-bit helper (NvrRtHelper.exe, NVR repository tools/nvr-rt-helper) and the game
only hands it triangles: MeasureGeometry / StageGeometry / FlushStagedGeometry convert D3D9 vertex
and index ranges (float3/float4/half4 positions, 16/32-bit lists and strips, base vertex, no index
buffer) by device address into a staging copy of an R32F shared image (the arena) and copy the
touched rows into it. Work on every GPU with the fork.

The in-process path (CreateGeometry / CreateScene / BuildScene, ray queries in NVR's own passes by
the scene's address) enables VK_KHR_acceleration_structure + VK_KHR_ray_query only with dxvk.conf
nvr.rayTracing = True and a driver that offers them to this process (Linux RADV); default off, the
Vulkan device is then created as without this patch.

DXVK edits: the three extensions and their features (dxvk_device_info), their entry points
(vulkan_loader.h), DxvkContext::nvrBeginRawCommands / nvrEndRawCommands / nvrFullBarrier /
nvrTrack*, DxvkCommandList::nvrExecBuffer, a friend line in D3D9DeviceEx (FlushBuffer).
New: d3d9_nvr_rt.{h,cpp}, shaders/d3d9_nvr_rt_convert.comp.
